# What one measurement of benchmarks/normalize/input costs the normalize stage.
# Recorded deliberately, read by crates/normalize/tests/bench.rs, and compared
# against the multiples in benchmarks/thresholds.
#
# allocations and bytes are counted, not timed: the stage asks for its output
# buffer once and reuses it across all 512 passes, and 11836 is the workload
# itself. Both numbers are the same on every machine.
allocations 1
bytes 11836

# The stage's time as a multiple of a plain copy of the same bytes, measured in
# the same run — the machine's own speed is in both halves and divides out, which
# is what lets a number recorded on one machine mean anything on another. Above
# one because the stage is a call the copy is not.
#
# Near the middle of what the machine that recorded it measured: about two
# hundred runs, ranging from 0.92 to 1.47. A third decimal here would be
# recording that noise, and it is the threshold beside it that decides anything,
# never this last digit.
cost 1.2
